152
00:18:29,760 --> 00:18:33,680
Half the species are birds, and make up, I don't know,

153
00:18:33,740 --> 00:18:33,860
about

154
00:18:35,152 --> 00:18:37,612
Third of the breeding birds that are actually in Finland.

155
00:18:38,292 --> 00:18:42,132
I think that's pretty much every single bat we have, but then just a few more

156
00:18:42,192 --> 00:18:42,712
mammals.

157
00:18:43,932 --> 00:18:46,952
A few reptiles and amphibians, but that's about half that we get in Finland.

158
00:18:48,992 --> 00:18:52,752
A few butterflies and a moth, couple more invertebrates, and then

159
00:18:53,432 --> 00:18:57,352
a bunch of vascular plants, I think a lot of which are orchids.

160
00:18:57,832 --> 00:18:58,692
So what you might expect.

161
00:19:00,892 --> 00:19:02,572
So there are sort of restrictions we're applying.

162
00:19:02,652 --> 00:19:06,492
So in some cases, we conceal the location of the records

163
00:19:06,532 --> 00:19:07,192
completely.

164
00:19:08,052 --> 00:19:09,532
But for most species, we're doing some sort of

165
00:19:09,572 --> 00:19:13,032
location generalization,

166
00:19:13,252 --> 00:19:16,252
so aggregating data to grids of one, five, 10,

167
00:19:17,412 --> 00:19:20,172
25, 50, 100-kilometer grid squares.

168
00:19:20,312 --> 00:19:24,272
And these restrictions might apply to the whole of Finland and the adjacent

169
00:19:24,352 --> 00:19:26,032
region when we're getting data,

170
00:19:26,892 --> 00:19:29,292
or sometimes it might be limited to

171
00:19:30,372 --> 00:19:34,292
a particular area of Finland, like the north, or a particular time of year,

172
00:19:34,372 --> 00:19:37,992
like records that occur during the breeding season or during wintering periods.

173
00:19:39,511 --> 00:19:42,412
And sometimes it might even apply to very specific locations, such as known

174
00:19:42,472 --> 00:19:44,572
breeding sites or nesting sites, stuff like that.

175
00:19:45,392 --> 00:19:47,912
We will often and do

176
00:19:48,812 --> 00:19:52,112
conceal other information that might inadvertently reveal the location of a

177
00:19:52,212 --> 00:19:55,252
species, like field notes for field records.

178
00:19:57,112 --> 00:20:01,032
And we also make an effort to unlink records that are

179
00:20:01,072 --> 00:20:04,532
part of multi-record events, such that you can't infer

180
00:20:05,392 --> 00:20:09,232
the location of a restricted species when you're looking at the

181
00:20:09,272 --> 00:20:12,032
location of an unrestricted species that was seen at the same time.

182
00:20:14,472 --> 00:20:18,272
So once those restrictions have been applied, the data is then available

183
00:20:18,672 --> 00:20:22,512
on our public data portal, which is a interface to our public data warehouse where

184
00:20:22,592 --> 00:20:25,332
all the Finnish biodiversity data has been harmonized.

185
00:20:26,232 --> 00:20:30,132
And you can access this via a rich web interface with filtering and analytics and

186
00:20:30,172 --> 00:20:31,992
all the sort of bells and whistles you might expect.

187
00:20:32,772 --> 00:20:36,572
And then it's available via our web API and some API clients, and with a

188
00:20:36,612 --> 00:20:39,252
free user account, you can do a bulk data download

189
00:20:41,912 --> 00:20:45,131
via a request service that produces referenceable

190
00:20:45,852 --> 00:20:46,492
material with

191
00:20:47,832 --> 00:20:51,612
persistent identifiers. Running in parallel, though, is a government authorities

192
00:20:51,652 --> 00:20:52,312
data portal,

193
00:20:53,612 --> 00:20:55,932
where you can get unrestricted access to data.

194
00:20:56,832 --> 00:20:57,812
But this requires

195
00:20:59,692 --> 00:21:03,592
a user account which has been limited to authorized employees of Finnish

196
00:21:03,652 --> 00:21:07,512
governments at all levels, national, regional, and municipal levels.

197
00:21:08,192 --> 00:21:12,112
And access to this data portal is logged so that we know who's accessing the

198
00:21:12,152 --> 00:21:12,852
data and when.

199
00:21:14,112 --> 00:21:17,132
But if you're a user that doesn't work for a Finnish government

200
00:21:18,752 --> 00:21:22,392
but needs access to this unrestricted data, you can use our data request service

201
00:21:22,472 --> 00:21:23,812
that allows you to

202
00:21:24,732 --> 00:21:24,952
make a

203
00:21:25,852 --> 00:21:29,132
request which might be fulfilled at the discretion of the data providers or their

204
00:21:29,192 --> 00:21:29,672
proxies.

205
00:21:31,452 --> 00:21:34,892
Requests can be made via the public data portal, but you need to provide a

206
00:21:34,952 --> 00:21:36,012
reason for

207
00:21:37,412 --> 00:21:40,792
needing the restricted data, such as you're doing a research project or something

208
00:21:40,832 --> 00:21:44,552
like that. And the system we have mediates these requests from users

209
00:21:44,652 --> 00:21:46,752
and the approvals of the providers.

210
00:21:47,952 --> 00:21:50,012
So you can make a single request for

211
00:21:51,072 --> 00:21:54,512
a bulk data download that will go out to multiple providers simultaneously

212
00:21:55,172 --> 00:21:57,092
and may or may not be fulfilled.

213
00:21:58,692 --> 00:21:58,992
So

214
00:22:01,772 --> 00:22:05,232
the majority of the data that we mediate in FinBIF is then

215
00:22:05,292 --> 00:22:08,952
published as Darwin Core Archives and indexed by GBIF.

216
00:22:11,532 --> 00:22:13,772
And these data have the data restrictions applied.

217
00:22:14,192 --> 00:22:14,532
So

218
00:22:15,412 --> 00:22:19,312
we use some of the Darwin Core standard to try and indicate something about what

219
00:22:19,612 --> 00:22:23,332
information we're withholding. So for occurrence class records, we

220
00:22:23,452 --> 00:22:25,972
use Darwin Core property information withheld,

221
00:22:28,052 --> 00:22:31,952
and the value of this will consist of a short statement that indicates what

222
00:22:31,992 --> 00:22:35,272
information is missing and by implication, what might be available on request.

223
00:22:36,252 --> 00:22:40,112
Then we'll use the property Darwin Core data generalizations

224
00:22:40,192 --> 00:22:43,812
to indicate the level of location aggregation

225
00:22:44,192 --> 00:22:45,272
we're using for records.

226
00:22:46,652 --> 00:22:50,292
But a lot of the information that we actually have about what restrictions we're

227
00:22:50,352 --> 00:22:52,792
doing and what a user might be able to

228
00:22:53,992 --> 00:22:54,972
ask for is not

229
00:22:56,552 --> 00:22:59,272
currently provided to us with any sort of data standard.

230
00:23:00,892 --> 00:23:04,652
We have a rarity species checklist, but it's not in any

231
00:23:04,752 --> 00:23:08,532
standard format. It's available online as an Excel

232
00:23:08,612 --> 00:23:09,112
