and the Wurrung / Woiwurrung people

785
00:54:31,016 --> 00:54:34,956
of the Kulin Nation. They're not from the lands that I work on, which is why I

786
00:54:34,996 --> 00:54:36,096
have problems pronouncing it.

787
00:54:37,216 --> 00:54:40,736
It always was Aboriginal land and always will be.

788
00:54:41,516 --> 00:54:44,616
We wish to acknowledge their elders, past and present, and to extend that

789
00:54:45,056 --> 00:54:48,016
acknowledgment to any First Nations people joining us today.

790
00:54:49,016 --> 00:54:49,236
So,

791
00:54:50,116 --> 00:54:52,876
the first few talks are actually a really good introduction to

792
00:54:54,236 --> 00:54:55,716
my talk, which

793
00:54:56,576 --> 00:55:00,076
is really about the complex edge cases that

794
00:55:00,456 --> 00:55:02,036
arise from implementing

795
00:55:03,296 --> 00:55:06,456
restricted access species solutions

796
00:55:07,216 --> 00:55:09,096
based on our experience in the ALA.

797
00:55:09,956 --> 00:55:11,416
So it's not necessarily

798
00:55:14,456 --> 00:55:18,076
a talk about solutions. It's a talk about some of the

799
00:55:18,116 --> 00:55:20,156
problems that we've come across in implementing.

800
00:55:24,516 --> 00:55:28,236
So as a refresher, and as you've probably gleaned from some of the earlier

801
00:55:28,316 --> 00:55:32,196
talks, there are a diverse range of reasons why species data

802
00:55:33,736 --> 00:55:36,296
can be considered restricted species data.

803
00:55:36,796 --> 00:55:40,576
This can range from more traditional issues like data sensitivity

804
00:55:40,656 --> 00:55:43,416
around publishing and withholding for conservation reasons,

805
00:55:44,416 --> 00:55:48,096
to more unusual reasons such as discouraging people from entering

806
00:55:48,236 --> 00:55:51,956
bombing ranges or the need to restrict access to biosecurity

807
00:55:52,016 --> 00:55:55,336
incursion eradications to avoid risks to

808
00:55:55,416 --> 00:55:56,616
agricultural trade.

809
00:55:58,960 --> 00:56:02,140
The ALA applies a variety of fuscations to over

810
00:56:02,840 --> 00:56:06,420
184 million records. We do everything from withholding

811
00:56:06,940 --> 00:56:10,880
records and images to generalizing records and even

812
00:56:10,920 --> 00:56:12,000
modifying fields.

813
00:56:13,100 --> 00:56:14,000
We use lists.

814
00:56:14,940 --> 00:56:15,160
The

815
00:56:16,460 --> 00:56:19,400
core of our system is basically lists.

816
00:56:19,680 --> 00:56:23,440
They're provided by government authorities at state, territory, or

817
00:56:23,500 --> 00:56:27,080
commonwealth level in Australia, and we use those to implement our

818
00:56:27,140 --> 00:56:27,880
solutions.

819
00:56:30,879 --> 00:56:33,840
We do also try to respect

820
00:56:34,680 --> 00:56:35,040
the

821
00:56:36,120 --> 00:56:39,440
variety of reasons that individual data providers give for applying

822
00:56:40,240 --> 00:56:41,360
treatments to their data.

823
00:56:44,000 --> 00:56:45,540
So our interest

824
00:56:47,140 --> 00:56:50,620
in this talk is really about how the intention of

825
00:56:50,680 --> 00:56:54,180
applying a RASD treatment to a data set can

826
00:56:54,220 --> 00:56:55,400
result in

827
00:56:56,720 --> 00:57:00,480
greater complexity in the solution than perhaps you intended by the

828
00:57:00,560 --> 00:57:00,980
simple

829
00:57:02,440 --> 00:57:06,400
issue of, for example, generalizing a record to 10 kilometers.

830
00:57:14,120 --> 00:57:17,160
So our principal take-home message that

831
00:57:17,940 --> 00:57:21,400
underpins all of these issues that we're going to raise is basically making sure

832
00:57:21,440 --> 00:57:25,000
you plan your approach. It's critical that when you consider

833
00:57:25,060 --> 00:57:25,660
treatments

834
00:57:27,220 --> 00:57:31,180
that you're going to be applying, you need to give attention to what's actually

835
00:57:31,260 --> 00:57:34,880
being restricted, what are the flow-on effects of the treatment,

836
00:57:35,280 --> 00:57:39,060
and what are the risks. And so I'm going to go through a few

837
00:57:39,120 --> 00:57:43,040
examples where taxonomy, data errors, associated

838
00:57:43,120 --> 00:57:46,280
data, and associated records all

839
00:57:46,340 --> 00:57:47,180
unexpectedly

840
00:57:48,220 --> 00:57:49,520
complicated our approach.

841
00:57:52,620 --> 00:57:55,680
Anyone who deals with data knows how challenging taxonomy is.

842
00:57:56,940 --> 00:58:00,560
And it is an absolute nightmare if you're dealing with RASD

843
00:58:00,940 --> 00:58:04,340
and you're trying to implement solutions based on

844
00:58:05,000 --> 00:58:08,980
a species concept. Most organizations implementing

845
00:58:09,040 --> 00:58:12,840
RASD use a list of species at some point that they regarded as restricted.

846
00:58:13,800 --> 00:58:17,700
As I've already said, we use lists given to us by various governments in

847
00:58:17,740 --> 00:58:20,600
Australia. But one of the biggest problems is

848
00:58:22,240 --> 00:58:26,020
not all RASD species in Australia are formally

849
00:58:26,060 --> 00:58:30,000
described. That gives you uncertainty around the name, both in

850
00:58:30,080 --> 00:58:33,720
terms of the list that you're trying to apply, but also the data that you're

851
00:58:33,780 --> 00:58:35,560
trying to match against that list.

852
00:58:36,680 --> 00:58:40,580
To complicate that matter, there's also the issue that not all

853
00:58:40,660 --> 00:58:44,360
states and territories agree on what species are at the actual

854
00:58:44,440 --> 00:58:45,520
current correct name,

855
00:58:47,120 --> 00:58:50,680
and that some data

856
00:58:50,740 --> 00:58:54,580
providers are contributing data at subspecific

857
00:58:54,680 --> 00:58:55,000
level

858
00:58:56,160 --> 00:58:57,120
but the

859
00:58:59,660 --> 00:59:03,140
sensitivity is being applied at species level or vice

860
00:59:03,280 --> 00:59:04,400
versa. So

861
00:59:05,360 --> 00:59:08,980
it's quite common in Australia to have an issue where a government

862
00:59:09,020 --> 00:59:12,340
authority that's telling us that a subspecies is sensitive

863
00:59:13,340 --> 00:59:16,360
is actually only supplying their data at species level.

864
00:59:16,440 --> 00:59:18,100
So there's literally no way for us,

865
00:59:19,140 --> 00:59:21,660
unless we intervene, to actually

866
00:59:22,960 --> 00:59:23,940
make that work.

867
00:59:26,280 --> 00:59:26,720
So

868
00:59:27,600 --> 00:59:31,520
what we're working towards is basically a situation

869
00:59:31,580 --> 00:59:33,320
where we try to account for

870
00:59:34,300 --> 00:59:38,000
that taxonomic variability by applying a precautionary

871
00:59:38,060 --> 00:59:38,560
principle.

872
00:59:39,900 --> 00:59:43,660
So our general rule of thumb is if we've got to apply a species, we

873
00:59:43,720 --> 00:59:47,400
can't resolve the taxonomic resolution, we tend to go up a

874
00:59:47,480 --> 00:59:50,840
taxonomic level, so we're protecting everything by default.

875
00:59:51,360 --> 00:59:53,100
It's pretty clumsy, but

876
00:59:54,980 --> 00:59:58,680
it's kind of really interesting because it's not necessarily something you pick up

877
00:59:58,740 --> 01:00:00,360
in the data unless you're looking for it.

878
01:00:07,420 --> 01:00:07,700
So

879
01:00:08,760 --> 01:00:12,400
I've just given three pretty common issues that we've

880
01:00:12,460 --> 01:00:15,860
encountered. So in the first example, the species we've been asked to

881
01:00:15,920 --> 01:00:18,700
generalize is not in the ALA's taxonomic backbone.

882
01:00:19,880 --> 01:00:23,760
This causes two issues. Firstly, there's no species to generalize

883
01:00:24,140 --> 01:00:27,960
because the ALA's entire system works off a taxonomic

884
01:00:28,000 --> 01:00:31,660
backbone. If the species isn't there, what are you going to attach your

885
01:00:31,720 --> 01:00:32,400
treatment to?

886
01:00:34,720 --> 01:00:38,180
In the second one, the data that needs to be generalized

887
01:00:38,320 --> 01:00:38,780
is

888
01:00:39,600 --> 01:00:41,240
likely to be matched at the next highest--

889
01:00:43,320 --> 01:00:47,220
sorry. The data that needs to be matched, i.e., the data that's coming

890
01:00:47,260 --> 01:00:51,180
into the system, because we don't have the species in the system, the data will

891
01:00:51,240 --> 01:00:53,320
attach at the next highest taxonomic level.

892
01:00:53,380 --> 01:00:57,300
So because it's not in the system as a species, it will attach to

893
01:00:57,340 --> 01:00:58,020
the genus.

894
01:00:59,260 --> 01:01:02,700
Therefore, our approach has to be generalizing any

895
01:01:02,760 --> 01:01:06,500
data that has come into the genus but has attached at

896
01:01:06,560 --> 01:01:10,380
genus level, not at species level, if that makes sense, I hope, because it's not

897
01:01:10,420 --> 01:01:12,700
very clear. In the second example,

898
01:01:13,640 --> 01:01:15,620
a subspecies needs to be generalized.

899
01:01:15,800 --> 01:01:19,620
However, as I've already said, some data sets are only supplied at

900
01:01:19,700 --> 01:01:21,720
species level. One possible

901
01:01:22,620 --> 01:01:26,500
approach is to approach the data providers and get them to

902
01:01:26,560 --> 01:01:30,220
supply data at subspecific level, which we've done in some

903
01:01:30,260 --> 01:01:30,860
instances.

904
01:01:31,840 --> 01:01:35,580
But generally, we've found that even if you go back to source, the

905
01:01:35,640 --> 01:01:39,240
data provider themselves doesn't actually have the data at a finer

906
01:01:39,320 --> 01:01:40,220
taxonomic level.

907
01:01:41,120 --> 01:01:41,500
So

908
01:01:42,420 --> 01:01:45,900
the solution is similar to the next example that I've

909
01:01:46,320 --> 01:01:50,020
got, the third example, where a non-taxonomic unit such as a

910
01:01:50,080 --> 01:01:53,560
population is identified. So what you've basically

911
01:01:54,180 --> 01:01:58,060
got to do is come up with a bespoke solution, either generalizing the

912
01:01:58,160 --> 01:02:02,140
species instead of the subspecies, even though the subspecies is the thing of

913
01:02:02,200 --> 01:02:05,960
concern. In the case of a population, the ALA

914
01:02:06,000 --> 01:02:09,740
has no way of delineating a population, so we just generalize the

915
01:02:09,780 --> 01:02:11,180
entire species

916
01:02:12,040 --> 01:02:15,900
rather than the population.

917
01:02:21,610 --> 01:02:25,390
We've also encountered problems with RASD where there are errors in the

918
01:02:25,430 --> 01:02:28,030
incoming data. These increasingly

919
01:02:28,890 --> 01:02:32,230
are a problem because AI makes these very easy to mine

920
01:02:32,790 --> 01:02:35,270
and return information that you thought was hidden or altered.

921
01:02:35,750 --> 01:02:39,150
For example, if you're applying treatments automatically to a polygon,

922
01:02:40,490 --> 01:02:43,990
such as a country or state boundary, what happens if a record has an

923
01:02:44,050 --> 01:02:46,230
incorrect latitude or longitude?

924
01:02:46,330 --> 01:02:49,590
So for example, if the rule set that you've got

925
01:02:50,150 --> 01:02:50,450
is

926
01:02:51,850 --> 01:02:55,810
you say any records inside this polygon

927
01:02:55,870 --> 01:02:59,690
need to be treated for RASD, and you've got an erroneous latitude and

928
01:02:59,730 --> 01:03:03,290
longitude that falls outside the polygon, what you end up with

929
01:03:03,490 --> 01:03:06,930
is a piece of RASD that is fully

930
01:03:06,990 --> 01:03:10,830
exposed because it's not actually hitting the preconditions that you've set for

931
01:03:10,870 --> 01:03:13,990
your data set, which is, again, really challenging.

932
01:03:15,710 --> 01:03:19,650
If you were applying RASD treatments based on a species list, what happens

933
01:03:19,710 --> 01:03:23,330
if the incoming data has a scientific name in the incorrect field?

934
01:03:23,790 --> 01:03:27,750
This is something we encounter all the time with eDNA data sets because

935
01:03:28,650 --> 01:03:32,350
a lot of eDNA data sets will have the bin number or something else

936
01:03:32,410 --> 01:03:36,390
coming in in the specific field, and it's been attached to the record

937
01:03:36,430 --> 01:03:38,030
in another field. So unless we're

938
01:03:39,010 --> 01:03:42,910
consistently dealing with those things, we can quite often end up exposing

939
01:03:42,970 --> 01:03:44,290
RASD inadvertently.

940
01:03:47,430 --> 01:03:51,210
Another common issue is where supplementary information has been

941
01:03:51,250 --> 01:03:55,170
included in the wrong field. We came across one the other day where we had a

942
01:03:55,210 --> 01:03:56,750
restricted access species.

943
01:03:57,850 --> 01:04:01,490
The incoming data set had simply recorded the

944
01:04:01,530 --> 01:04:03,610
locality information in the wrong field.

945
01:04:04,710 --> 01:04:04,890
And

946
01:04:06,130 --> 01:04:09,550
we had an experimental AI thing that was mining

947
01:04:09,650 --> 01:04:12,390
iNaturalist data for this particular species.

948
01:04:13,770 --> 01:04:16,410
Yep, and I'm going to start singing bad Australian rock now.

949
01:04:18,730 --> 01:04:22,030
And effectively what was happening was our own system

950
01:04:22,810 --> 01:04:26,550
was basically exposing the data purely because

951
01:04:26,630 --> 01:04:29,190
the locational field was in the wrong field.

952
01:04:30,370 --> 01:04:34,070
I do have some other examples. I'm going to leave it there because I'm being a good

953
01:04:34,130 --> 01:04:36,110
citizen. And thank you.

954
01:04:44,590 --> 01:04:45,650
So our next speaker,

955
01:04:47,710 --> 01:04:48,350
is there? Good,

956
01:04:49,210 --> 01:04:49,430
is

957
01:04:50,630 --> 01:04:54,530
Charlotte Lassaline, and I am just

958
01:04:54,590 --> 01:04:56,250
trying to get her presentation up.

959
01:04:56,650 --> 01:04:59,130
She is going to be speaking on

960
01:05:00,570 --> 01:05:03,610
the really fascinating issue of...

961
01:05:06,590 --> 01:05:09,490
Sorry, I'm just trying to get your presentation to appear.

962
01:05:21,530 --> 01:05:23,270
Bloop. Apologies.

963
01:05:24,450 --> 01:05:27,970
Charlotte's going to be speaking on the fascinating issue of

964
01:05:30,610 --> 01:05:32,210
wildlife trade species,

965
01:05:33,210 --> 01:05:36,210
and the work that she's been doing in Australia, which has been absolutely

966
01:05:36,290 --> 01:05:39,910
fascinating. And I seem to be having a problem with-

967
01:05:40,050 --> 01:05:53,830
Yeah.

968
01:05:53,850 --> 01:05:54,250
Thank you.

969
01:05:56,090 --> 01:05:56,790
Thank you, Charlotte.

970
01:05:57,170 --> 01:05:59,130
Thank you. A lot of suspense there.

971
01:06:00,990 --> 01:06:04,870
Hi, my name's Charlotte. I am a PhD researcher at Adelaide

972
01:06:04,930 --> 01:06:08,450
University, and I research illegal wildlife trade in Australia,

973
01:06:08,630 --> 01:06:10,610
particularly the trade of invertebrates.

974
01:06:14,350 --> 01:06:18,310
So wildlife trade is a global conservation issue, and its scale is quite

975
01:06:18,370 --> 01:06:22,330
substantial. Currently, it is predicted to affect one in every five

976
01:06:22,430 --> 01:06:23,450
species worldwide.

977
01:06:24,670 --> 01:06:28,590
At the same time, we're living in an era of unprecedented biodiversity data

978
01:06:28,650 --> 01:06:29,310
availability.

979
01:06:30,310 --> 01:06:34,250
Open access biodiversity platforms and scientific literature have made

980
01:06:34,330 --> 01:06:37,090
species information more accessible now than ever before,

981
01:06:38,030 --> 01:06:41,790
which is incredibly valuable for conservation, for research, and for

982
01:06:41,850 --> 01:06:45,350
public engagement, but it also creates conservation risks.

983
01:06:46,410 --> 01:06:50,090
Poachers have been known to use public occurrence data to locate and

984
01:06:50,150 --> 01:06:53,490
target species for collection. And so this raises the important

985
01:06:53,550 --> 01:06:56,970
question of how we can balance the principles of open and accessible

986
01:06:57,010 --> 01:07:00,950
biodiversity data with the need to protect species that are vulnerable to

987
01:07:00,990 --> 01:07:02,370
this level of exploitation.

988
01:07:03,670 --> 01:07:07,570
In this talk, I'll explore how wildlife trade data can

989
01:07:07,610 --> 01:07:11,530
be used to inform this balance, and I'll be looking at it from the Australian

990
01:07:11,570 --> 01:07:13,770
context, where I'm researching.

991
01:07:19,530 --> 01:07:19,910
Okay.

992
01:07:23,810 --> 01:07:27,450
So how do we decide what species should be included in these

993
01:07:27,510 --> 01:07:28,570
RASD frameworks?

994
01:07:29,710 --> 01:07:32,570
And how do we decide what species have their locality data restricted?

995
01:07:33,790 --> 01:07:37,590
So RASD lists or sensitive species lists are managed in a

996
01:07:37,630 --> 01:07:40,610
wide range of different ways right around the world, as we're hearing today.

997
01:07:41,370 --> 01:07:44,490
But largely, they're informed by species that have had their

998
01:07:44,910 --> 01:07:46,890
conservation formally assessed.

999
01:07:47,950 --> 01:07:51,030
And so, for example, this could be through IUCN Red Lists, as we've heard about,

1000
01:07:51,570 --> 01:07:55,150
through CITES appendices as well, and through government species

1001
01:07:55,190 --> 01:07:56,070
protection lists.

1002
01:07:57,150 --> 01:08:01,090
But doing these formal conservation assessments can be resource-intensive,

1003
01:08:01,650 --> 01:08:05,470
time-consuming, and most often they require quite a bit

1004
01:08:05,550 --> 01:08:07,530
of knowledge and data on a species.

1005
01:08:08,850 --> 01:08:12,590
So this creates a limitation where species that are poorly studied or have limited

1006
01:08:12,630 --> 01:08:16,550
available data on them are less likely to receive this level of

1007
01:08:16,630 --> 01:08:20,530
formal recognition, even when they may be vulnerable to exploitation

1008
01:08:20,570 --> 01:08:21,410
through wildlife trade.

1009
01:08:23,422 --> 01:08:27,321
So as a result, existing conservation assessments and consequently approaches

1010
01:08:27,382 --> 01:08:31,382
to selecting species to be considered for RASD frameworks can be

1011
01:08:31,422 --> 01:08:34,862
biased towards taxa for which we have more information on them, and this is

1012
01:08:34,902 --> 01:08:36,242
particularly towards vertebrates.

1013
01:08:38,742 --> 01:08:41,942
And these biases become quite clear when you look at the taxonomic coverage of

1014
01:08:42,043 --> 01:08:44,062
IUCN's Red List of Threatened Species.

1015
01:08:44,682 --> 01:08:48,442
So this is the animals that are covered by IUCN's Red List as of this

1016
01:08:48,522 --> 01:08:48,722
year.

1017
01:08:50,022 --> 01:08:53,222
So for vertebrates, as you can see, the vast majority of them have had their

1018
01:08:53,262 --> 01:08:56,402
conservation status evaluated, whereas only

1019
01:08:56,802 --> 01:09:00,342
1.4% of the insects and just over 1% of the arachnids

1020
01:09:00,782 --> 01:09:03,222
have had their conservation status evaluated.

1021
01:09:04,123 --> 01:09:08,062
And this disparity is particularly striking when you look at

1022
01:09:08,102 --> 01:09:11,782
the huge number of species that are represented by these bottom two groups here.

1023
01:09:13,543 --> 01:09:17,302
And so if we're relying primarily on these existing conservation

1024
01:09:17,362 --> 01:09:21,062
frameworks or existing conservation assessments to identify species

1025
01:09:21,762 --> 01:09:25,442
for these frameworks, we risk overlooking a very large proportion of

1026
01:09:25,503 --> 01:09:25,963
species.

1027
01:09:28,522 --> 01:09:31,522
Not necessarily because they're less vulnerable, but simply because we don't have

1028
01:09:31,543 --> 01:09:33,782
the same level of formal assessment available for them.

1029
01:09:34,642 --> 01:09:38,463
And so this raises the question, can we identify species that may warrant this

1030
01:09:38,503 --> 01:09:42,222
kind of data protection before they have undergone these lengthy formal assessment

1031
01:09:42,262 --> 01:09:45,842
processes? And this is particularly important when we consider

1032
01:09:45,922 --> 01:09:46,543
invertebrates.

1033
01:09:47,982 --> 01:09:48,562
Invertebrates,

1034
01:09:50,762 --> 01:09:54,462
a lot of them are newly described, they're largely range-restricted,

1035
01:09:54,842 --> 01:09:58,802
many of them are poorly known in general, they're data deficient, and as

1036
01:09:58,822 --> 01:10:02,342
we've just seen, they are poorly represented across conservation frameworks and

1037
01:10:02,382 --> 01:10:03,662
assessments right around the world.

1038
01:10:07,302 --> 01:10:10,642
At the same time, we're seeing an increasing interest in invertebrates in wildlife

1039
01:10:10,702 --> 01:10:11,002
trade.

1040
01:10:12,082 --> 01:10:15,582
This leads to a situation where some of the species that are the most poorly

1041
01:10:15,642 --> 01:10:19,422
understood in terms of research and conservation are also

1042
01:10:19,442 --> 01:10:21,842
becoming increasingly sought after by collectors.

1043
01:10:23,282 --> 01:10:25,662
And this creates another important problem for RASD,

1044
01:10:26,582 --> 01:10:29,942
where for many of these species, the publicly available occurrence data provides

1045
01:10:29,982 --> 01:10:33,122
some of the only accessible information we have about where they're occurring.

1046
01:10:34,042 --> 01:10:37,662
So the very data that is essential for understanding and conserving these species

1047
01:10:37,702 --> 01:10:41,462
can also potentially make them easier to locate and exploit for poachers.

1048
01:10:43,102 --> 01:10:46,102
So this raises the question of whether we can use information that we have

1049
01:10:46,142 --> 01:10:49,902
available on a species' trade and demand in wildlife trade to

1050
01:10:49,962 --> 01:10:53,722
identify these risks before a species has the opportunity to undergo

1051
01:10:53,762 --> 01:10:57,442
these formal conservation assessments and to be formally recognized as threatened.

1052
01:11:00,462 --> 01:11:03,582
So this brings me to the broader governance problem, where these formal

1053
01:11:03,622 --> 01:11:06,062
conservation assessments usually happen quite slowly.

1054
01:11:06,542 --> 01:11:09,942
And even once they've happened, translating that into legal protection can take

1055
01:11:09,982 --> 01:11:10,482
even longer.

1056
01:11:11,622 --> 01:11:15,502
But wildlife trade doesn't wait for these processes to catch up, and a species

1057
01:11:15,542 --> 01:11:19,502
can become desirable in trade and exploitation can begin long before a

1058
01:11:19,602 --> 01:11:21,382
species is even recognized as vulnerable.

1059
01:11:23,342 --> 01:11:25,782
And this creates a potential window of vulnerability where a

1060
01:11:26,042 --> 01:11:29,942
species is exposed to exploitation, but there are very

1061
01:11:30,002 --> 01:11:32,262
few mechanisms in place to protect the species.

1062
01:11:33,262 --> 01:11:36,962
So what happens in the period before a species becomes officially recognized as

1063
01:11:37,002 --> 01:11:40,562
vulnerable? And can we use information that's already available to us to

1064
01:11:40,602 --> 01:11:44,182
identify species that warrant data protection during that period?

1065
01:11:47,862 --> 01:11:51,582
So rather than only asking, is a species recognized as being

1066
01:11:51,622 --> 01:11:55,602
threatened, I propose we also ask a different question: Is a species

1067
01:11:55,642 --> 01:11:58,142
showing early signs of exploitation through wildlife trade?

1068
01:11:59,582 --> 01:12:03,222
Wildlife trade data is something that is available right around the world across

1069
01:12:03,262 --> 01:12:06,602
different regions and taxonomic groups, and is largely within the scientific

1070
01:12:06,662 --> 01:12:07,122
literature.

1071
01:12:07,942 --> 01:12:10,522
And it can provide some useful indicators of this.

1072
01:12:10,662 --> 01:12:14,362
It can tell us about consumer demand, emerging and shifting

1073
01:12:14,422 --> 01:12:18,242
market trends, as this is always changing across wildlife trade, and also

1074
01:12:18,282 --> 01:12:22,182
particular species' traits and characteristics that are appearing

1075
01:12:22,222 --> 01:12:23,782
to be desirable to collectors.

1076
01:12:25,402 --> 01:12:29,042
Also, trade data can quite often provide solid evidence that species are being

1077
01:12:29,102 --> 01:12:30,262
sourced right from the wild.

1078
01:12:32,022 --> 01:12:35,022
So importantly, this means the trade pressure can be considered as an early

1079
01:12:35,062 --> 01:12:38,822
indicator of exploitation risk. It doesn't necessarily tell us that a

1080
01:12:38,902 --> 01:12:42,362
species is vulnerable, but it tells us that a species is being exposed to a

1081
01:12:42,402 --> 01:12:45,722
pressure that may lead to it being threatened in the very near future.

1082
01:12:46,782 --> 01:12:50,462
And this is a concept I want to introduce today, using wildlife trade

1083
01:12:50,522 --> 01:12:53,162
data as a conservation early warning system.

1084
01:12:56,002 --> 01:12:59,462
So a good example of this and of how these RASD frameworks are working in

1085
01:12:59,502 --> 01:13:00,922
Australia is with the

1086
01:13:03,242 --> 01:13:05,082
Kangaroo Island pygmy trapdoor spider.

1087
01:13:06,042 --> 01:13:09,662
This is a spider that is highly range-restricted, has a small population,

1088
01:13:10,062 --> 01:13:13,162
and it lives on Kangaroo Island right at the bottom of South Australia.

1089
01:13:14,962 --> 01:13:18,382
Following recent bushfires, it was nationally declared as

1090
01:13:18,442 --> 01:13:19,882
endangered and,

1091
01:13:20,762 --> 01:13:24,642
despite its conservation significance, its occurrence data was

1092
01:13:24,702 --> 01:13:25,742
publicly available still.

1093
01:13:27,122 --> 01:13:31,102
Early this year, a poaching incident was reported by researchers that had

1094
01:13:31,142 --> 01:13:34,662
gone out to their known locations where they had observed their burrows being dug

1095
01:13:34,842 --> 01:13:35,642
up, and it was

1096
01:13:36,902 --> 01:13:39,482
quite easy to tell that this was a human disturbance.

1097
01:13:40,402 --> 01:13:44,142
And so using images of this human disturbance and this poaching

1098
01:13:44,222 --> 01:13:48,142
incident, we were able to report it to ALA, and very quickly,

1099
01:13:49,002 --> 01:13:52,422
they helped us further that report to relevant state government.

1100
01:13:53,842 --> 01:13:57,102
Very quickly, their data was made restricted online.

1101
01:13:57,642 --> 01:14:01,142
This happened within a span of, I reckon it was less than two weeks, so it was a

1102
01:14:01,202 --> 01:14:04,562
very effective and quick approach. Data restricted.

1103
01:14:05,002 --> 01:14:08,882
And can we identify species that are highly likely to be vulnerable to this

1104
01:14:08,942 --> 01:14:12,482
type of exploitation before the exploitation is actually caught in the act?

1105
01:14:15,622 --> 01:14:18,342
So the key difference in this approach is the timing.

1106
01:14:19,542 --> 01:14:23,122
A reactive approach, as I just talked about, it responds once a threat is

1107
01:14:23,782 --> 01:14:27,022
confirmed or the exploitation has already occurred.

1108
01:14:28,134 --> 01:14:32,054
A proactive approach looks for an early warning signal, such as emerging trade

1109
01:14:32,094 --> 01:14:35,854
pressure or collected interest, before significant exploitation actually

1110
01:14:35,894 --> 01:14:36,274
occurs.

1111
01:14:37,234 --> 01:14:41,114
So the goal is to move from reacting to observed threats, towards

1112
01:14:41,174 --> 01:14:45,094
identifying potential risks early enough to prevent damage to these already

1113
01:14:45,234 --> 01:14:47,614
small and sometimes fragile populations.

1114
01:14:49,954 --> 01:14:53,554
So how can we actually use this wildlife trade data to identify

1115
01:14:53,594 --> 01:14:57,034
vulnerable species that haven't been formally assessed yet?

1116
01:14:58,534 --> 01:15:01,674
So the approach we've developed, what we're calling a wildlife trade

1117
01:15:02,254 --> 01:15:03,914
species sensitivity framework.

1118
01:15:05,074 --> 01:15:09,014
It starts with wildlife trade data, and this most often will include some

1119
01:15:09,074 --> 01:15:12,774
type of measure of trade demand. So this could be the number of advertisements, the

1120
01:15:12,814 --> 01:15:15,454
number of individuals that are being traded.

1121
01:15:15,974 --> 01:15:19,514
It could be the market value as well, and it combines this data

1122
01:15:19,594 --> 01:15:21,814
with biological sensitivity factors.

1123
01:15:22,034 --> 01:15:25,714
This could be anything from a species distribution, its population size,

1124
01:15:26,254 --> 01:15:28,614
reproductive output, dispersal ability.

1125
01:15:29,394 --> 01:15:30,514
I could go on for a long time.

1126
01:15:31,754 --> 01:15:35,174
These biological sensitivity factors, they're all individually weighted,

1127
01:15:35,814 --> 01:15:39,274
and what this framework spits out is a vulnerability index for each

1128
01:15:39,354 --> 01:15:39,854
species.

1129
01:15:40,714 --> 01:15:43,094
This allows us to identify species that

1130
01:15:44,074 --> 01:15:47,594
should be a high priority for further investigation and data protection.

1131
01:15:49,374 --> 01:15:52,934
We can then ask, are these species already formally assessed or

1132
01:15:52,974 --> 01:15:55,494
protected within Australia, for example?

1133
01:15:56,114 --> 01:16:00,054
If they are, then they should be considered for

1134
01:16:00,914 --> 01:16:03,434
restricted access species listings.

1135
01:16:04,474 --> 01:16:07,554
And if not, they should definitely be a candidate for further

1136
01:16:07,854 --> 01:16:08,694
investigation.

1137
01:16:10,254 --> 01:16:13,454
So in this way, trade data can act as an early warning mechanism to support

1138
01:16:13,494 --> 01:16:17,154
proactive RASD decision-making rather than waiting for this

1139
01:16:17,234 --> 01:16:19,234
exploitation and poaching to occur first.

1140
01:16:21,174 --> 01:16:24,674
So importantly, this isn't just about closing off access to

1141
01:16:24,714 --> 01:16:27,974
biodiversity data, but it's about finding the balance between

1142
01:16:28,034 --> 01:16:31,674
accessibility and also erring on the side of caution for

1143
01:16:31,754 --> 01:16:35,494
species protection, particularly in the absence of so much

1144
01:16:35,554 --> 01:16:37,074
data and knowledge on these species.

1145
01:16:38,234 --> 01:16:42,194
For vulnerable species, sensitive locality data would ideally be

1146
01:16:42,234 --> 01:16:45,894
obscured, not removed, while retaining access by request

1147
01:16:46,354 --> 01:16:50,274
and allowing for the restrictions to be renewed and reviewed as

1148
01:16:50,314 --> 01:16:52,214
more research comes out about these species.

1149
01:16:54,034 --> 01:16:57,534
Because for some species, particularly for the range-restricted invertebrates that

1150
01:16:57,594 --> 01:17:00,934
I've talked about today, the removal of even a few individuals from their

1151
01:17:00,974 --> 01:17:03,574
population could have significant consequences on them.

1152
01:17:04,554 --> 01:17:07,974
So the goal is therefore to use whatever data we have available

1153
01:17:08,514 --> 01:17:12,314
to identify potential risks early enough to protect these vulnerable species,

1154
01:17:12,854 --> 01:17:15,814
rather than waiting until the damage and the poaching has already been done.

1155
01:17:16,254 --> 01:17:16,594
Thanks.

1156
01:17:25,714 --> 01:17:26,334
Thanks, Charlotte.

1157
01:17:49,114 --> 01:17:49,154
Thanks.

1158
01:17:49,594 --> 01:17:49,614
So, I guess that's it.

1159
01:17:49,614 --> 01:17:49,614
Yep.

