3153
03:29:58,470 --> 03:30:01,290
So this is some work that I did with a couple of colleagues at Kew,

3154
03:30:02,430 --> 03:30:03,290
Ashley and Stephen.

3155
03:30:04,350 --> 03:30:07,630
They're both available online if you want to have a chat to any of us about this.

3156
03:30:08,290 --> 03:30:10,490
So we called this project Geonomia, and that's to

3157
03:30:11,730 --> 03:30:15,650
recognize the work that Bionomia has done in looking at the data that's been

3158
03:30:15,690 --> 03:30:19,330
mobilized through GBIF and disambiguating the people.

3159
03:30:20,290 --> 03:30:24,150
And for us, it's important also to disambiguate the geographic names that are

3160
03:30:24,910 --> 03:30:26,070
associated with our data.

3161
03:30:26,890 --> 03:30:30,530
And the reason we need to do this is because at Kew, we've digitized our

3162
03:30:30,590 --> 03:30:34,270
specimens, so we've got 6.3 million specimens now.

3163
03:30:35,470 --> 03:30:36,510
That's a bit of an old slide.

3164
03:30:38,350 --> 03:30:41,950
Yeah, and we've mobilized them into GBIF, and we have this lovely map.

3165
03:30:43,370 --> 03:30:45,050
But if you look-- Well, okay.

3166
03:30:45,970 --> 03:30:47,990
Yeah, look at that bit of the page, right?

3167
03:30:48,290 --> 03:30:52,070
So that map only being 13% of our data

3168
03:30:52,470 --> 03:30:52,790
because

3169
03:30:54,090 --> 03:30:57,350
we digitized the specimens by photographing them and then entering the metadata

3170
03:30:57,410 --> 03:30:59,090
from the specimen sheet,

3171
03:31:00,670 --> 03:31:03,550
and lots of our records, they don't have

3172
