applications are providing to users of their services.

4050
05:49:52,158 --> 05:49:55,958
Now, I just want to emphasize that the data added to Wikidata in

4051
05:49:56,038 --> 05:49:59,538
line with the proposed type specimen data model is not

4052
05:49:59,598 --> 05:50:03,298
intended to be canonical. What we are aiming to do is use

4053
05:50:03,358 --> 05:50:07,318
Wikidata as a finding aid to facilitate access to

4054
05:50:07,338 --> 05:50:11,078
the relevant data about type specimens and their related

4055
05:50:11,158 --> 05:50:14,878
identifiers. We aim for Wikidata to act as a bridge

4056
05:50:14,918 --> 05:50:18,458
between the different sources of information about the type specimen

4057
05:50:18,818 --> 05:50:22,438
and also provide links to the canonical data at source.

4058
05:50:22,858 --> 05:50:22,958
That

4059
05:50:24,398 --> 05:50:27,678
is to the data being provided by the holding institution.

4060
05:50:31,886 --> 05:50:34,386
So how do we go about creating this proposed data model?

4061
05:50:34,626 --> 05:50:38,586
Well, the process included comparing existing items on Wikidata

4062
05:50:38,646 --> 05:50:41,266
and considering the properties being used by editors.

4063
05:50:42,726 --> 05:50:46,186
We also had a look at the data standards created by this informatics

4064
05:50:46,246 --> 05:50:47,966
community, including Darwin Core.

4065
05:50:48,446 --> 05:50:50,566
We took a pragmatic approach.

4066
05:50:51,406 --> 05:50:55,306
As I know you'll be aware, many institutions upload this type of data to

4067
05:50:55,406 --> 05:50:58,986
aggregators like GBIF. We wanted to make it easy for

4068
05:50:59,066 --> 05:51:02,686
individuals associated with these types of institutions to

4069
05:51:02,786 --> 05:51:06,586
also share the data related to type specimens with Wikidata.

4070
05:51:07,306 --> 05:51:11,186
Another major consideration was how data might be extracted

4071
05:51:11,246 --> 05:51:15,186
from Wikidata. We thought about the types of queries the biodiversity

4072
05:51:15,246 --> 05:51:19,126
community might ask or want visualized and modeled the

4073
05:51:19,186 --> 05:51:22,006
data to ensure that these queries could be easily created.

4074
05:51:22,866 --> 05:51:25,706
We proposed several new Wikidata properties.

4075
05:51:26,126 --> 05:51:29,826
This included new properties for the collector, the specimen classifier,

4076
05:51:30,326 --> 05:51:32,706
the collection date, and the location collected.

4077
05:51:33,186 --> 05:51:37,025
And we were attempting to rectify the use of some Wikidata properties

4078
05:51:37,126 --> 05:51:41,106
as workarounds and wanted to ensure that pivotal data could be added

4079
05:51:41,166 --> 05:51:43,506
to the Wikidata type specimen item.

4080
05:51:44,346 --> 05:51:48,026
Finally, we created a batch of Wikidata items for holotype

4081
05:51:48,126 --> 05:51:51,706
specimens held at a New Zealand nationally significant collection,

4082
05:51:52,046 --> 05:51:53,546
the New Zealand Arthropod Collection.

4083
05:51:54,166 --> 05:51:57,846
The trial batch upload was based on a holotype specimen data

4084
05:51:57,906 --> 05:52:01,646
extracted from GBIF, placed in the data curation tool OpenRefine,

4085
05:52:02,166 --> 05:52:05,866
cleaned, and then reconciled with and uploaded into Wikidata,

4086
05:52:06,266 --> 05:52:08,986
and this batch helped us test and refine the model.

4087
05:52:12,086 --> 05:52:16,066
Now, as well as existing type specimen items and the likely queries of Wikidata,

4088
05:52:16,166 --> 05:52:20,086
the model was also informed by attempting to crosswalk properties used

4089
05:52:20,126 --> 05:52:22,606
in it to the concepts used in Darwin Core.

4090
05:52:22,926 --> 05:52:26,326
And this crosswalking really helped us learn about how institutions

4091
05:52:26,386 --> 05:52:30,206
aligned type specimen data with Darwin Core and then share this

4092
05:52:30,266 --> 05:52:33,766
data with GBIF, and it helped us think about the appropriate Wikidata

4093
05:52:33,826 --> 05:52:37,786
properties that were needed. It also encouraged us to

4094
05:52:37,826 --> 05:52:40,586
consider how the proposed model might be extended.

4095
05:52:43,906 --> 05:52:46,826
So where do you find the model and its associated documentation?

4096
05:52:46,986 --> 05:52:50,806
Well, we created a Wikidata Wiki Project page to aid collaboration with

4097
05:52:50,886 --> 05:52:54,746
the wider Wiki community. The Wiki project contains not just

4098
05:52:54,786 --> 05:52:58,226
the data model, it also contains supporting documentation and the

4099
