I'm a botanist and data scientist based at the Museum für

4200
05:58:37,116 --> 05:58:40,456
Naturkunde, where I head the research group Data for Nature.

4201
05:58:40,996 --> 05:58:44,576
And today I'm here to speak on behalf of the task group

4202
05:58:44,936 --> 05:58:46,676
Modeling Research Expeditions.

4203
05:58:47,696 --> 05:58:51,516
As you can see here, several of my co-authors are members of that task group,

4204
05:58:52,336 --> 05:58:53,496
but we are a larger group.

4205
05:59:01,176 --> 05:59:04,656
As you all know, research expeditions

4206
05:59:05,176 --> 05:59:09,076
are a major source of specimens and natural history collections,

4207
05:59:09,736 --> 05:59:13,656
connecting distributed cultural heritage across institutions and

4208
05:59:13,716 --> 05:59:14,216
countries.

4209
05:59:15,276 --> 05:59:18,876
Historically, these expeditions were often transdisciplinary.

4210
05:59:19,376 --> 05:59:22,796
Biological and earth science specimens were collected

4211
05:59:22,876 --> 05:59:26,816
alongside ethnographical and anthropological objects.

4212
05:59:27,636 --> 05:59:31,216
As a result, specimens and other materials gathered

4213
05:59:31,276 --> 05:59:35,116
during the same expedition, together with their associated

4214
05:59:35,196 --> 05:59:38,796
data and metadata, are often distributed across

4215
05:59:38,876 --> 05:59:40,316
multiple institutions.

4216
05:59:41,836 --> 05:59:42,556
Consequently,

4217
05:59:43,416 --> 05:59:46,956
expedition data remain fragmented, inconsistently

4218
05:59:47,036 --> 05:59:50,236
modeled, and often lack persistent identifiers.

4219
05:59:51,056 --> 05:59:54,996
This hampers discoverability, interoperability, and provenance

4220
05:59:55,076 --> 05:59:55,536
tracking.

4221
05:59:56,536 --> 06:00:00,476
It also makes it more difficult to contextualize collecting

4222
06:00:00,556 --> 06:00:04,276
events and practices within the broader historical context,

4223
06:00:05,656 --> 06:00:07,376
including colonial contexts.

4224
06:00:08,596 --> 06:00:12,416
Research expeditions continue to contribute to natural history

4225
06:00:12,456 --> 06:00:16,436
collections today. There's therefore a need to establish

4226
06:00:16,516 --> 06:00:20,236
clear links between historical, contemporary expeditions, and the

4227
06:00:20,316 --> 06:00:23,896
entities connected to these events, such as specimens,

4228
06:00:23,996 --> 06:00:26,436
publications, people, and institutions.

4229
06:00:28,256 --> 06:00:31,816
This requires unambiguous identification and

4230
06:00:31,916 --> 06:00:35,536
persistent identifiers for the expeditions themselves.

4231
06:00:41,816 --> 06:00:45,596
So what actually constitutes an expedition, and how do we

4232
06:00:45,656 --> 06:00:47,216
represent it in our data?

4233
06:00:48,276 --> 06:00:49,176
Terminology is

4234
06:00:50,015 --> 06:00:53,395
an important topic, and especially here in the context of a standard

4235
06:00:53,455 --> 06:00:57,156
organization. We have already heard from David and

4236
06:00:57,256 --> 06:01:00,916
others about the Darwin Core terms and the Darwin Core data

4237
06:01:00,976 --> 06:01:04,636
packages and the agent term and the event,

4238
06:01:05,396 --> 06:01:09,236
and also the parent event ID is relevant in

4239
06:01:09,316 --> 06:01:10,296
our context here.

4240
06:01:11,616 --> 06:01:15,096
The terms research expeditions and scientific expeditions

4241
06:01:15,896 --> 06:01:18,216
are themselves not unproblematic.

4242
06:01:19,056 --> 06:01:22,796
We recognize that such expeditions often took place within

4243
06:01:22,876 --> 06:01:26,296
complex historical contexts and could be closely

4244
06:01:26,376 --> 06:01:30,156
intertwined with colonial agendas and economic

4245
06:01:30,236 --> 06:01:32,156
and/or military interests.

4246
06:01:36,656 --> 06:01:40,076
We have created a working definition for research

4247
06:01:40,136 --> 06:01:41,936
expeditions, which you can see here.

4248
06:01:44,736 --> 06:01:48,516
A research expedition is a specific organized field activity

4249
06:01:48,916 --> 06:01:52,656
involving one or more people at one or more locations,

4250
06:01:53,116 --> 06:01:56,416
undertaken in affiliation with or in support of an

4251
06:01:56,476 --> 06:02:00,016
institution for the purpose of conducting, supporting, or

4252
06:02:00,076 --> 06:02:04,036
advancing research within a defined start and

4253
06:02:04,096 --> 06:02:04,576
end date.

4254
06:02:06,016 --> 06:02:09,316
An expedition is a distinct time-bounded activity

4255
06:02:09,996 --> 06:02:13,656
that can be distinguished from ongoing or routine collecting

4256
06:02:13,736 --> 06:02:14,456
activities.

4257
06:02:15,356 --> 06:02:18,316
It has a defined research aim,

4258
06:02:18,936 --> 06:02:20,056
although research

4259
06:02:21,876 --> 06:02:24,996
is not always the sole purpose. And yes,

4260
06:02:25,236 --> 06:02:29,176
obviously there are and there will always be edge cases where it is hard

4261
06:02:29,216 --> 06:02:32,736
to differentiate, and this is a draft definition on which we are still working.

4262
06:02:39,088 --> 06:02:42,508
So in GBIF, an expedition can already be

4263
06:02:42,668 --> 06:02:43,988
represented as an event,

4264
06:02:45,728 --> 06:02:49,708
and the parent event ID can be used to link collecting events to a

4265
06:02:49,748 --> 06:02:53,588
broader expedition event. In some data sets, Wikidata

4266
06:02:53,768 --> 06:02:57,468
QIDs are already used as identifiers for

4267
06:02:57,508 --> 06:02:58,788
these parent events.

4268
06:02:59,888 --> 06:03:03,728
Our task group is currently exploring and promoting the use

4269
06:03:03,788 --> 06:03:07,348
of Wikidata QIDs as globally unique resolvable

4270
06:03:07,428 --> 06:03:09,408
identifiers for expedition events

4271
06:03:10,268 --> 06:03:14,148
where appropriate. And in the longer term, we are considering whether

4272
06:03:14,208 --> 06:03:18,168
more explicit support for research expeditions in Darwin Core would be useful.

4273
06:03:18,428 --> 06:03:21,648
But this is also something we have to compare and discuss

4274
06:03:22,248 --> 06:03:22,728
also with the

4275
06:03:24,148 --> 06:03:25,468
Darwin Core data package.

4276
06:03:27,048 --> 06:03:30,648
The key idea is, however, to treat the expedition itself as a

4277
06:03:30,708 --> 06:03:34,468
first-class entity that can be identified and linked to

4278
06:03:34,568 --> 06:03:35,328
other entities.

4279
06:03:38,508 --> 06:03:42,288
So this work is part of the research expedition task group, which

4280
06:03:42,668 --> 06:03:44,988
is part of the collection description interest group,

4281
06:03:46,008 --> 06:03:49,828
and the group focuses on developing a model for structured,

4282
06:03:50,048 --> 06:03:53,268
linked open data describing research expeditions.

4283
06:03:53,768 --> 06:03:57,388
The aim is to develop a model that can work across institutions,

4284
06:03:57,788 --> 06:04:00,048
collections, and data systems.

4285
06:04:00,928 --> 06:04:04,828
Our goals include identifying stable identifiers and creating them

4286
06:04:04,888 --> 06:04:08,188
for research expeditions, developing guidelines

4287
06:04:09,268 --> 06:04:13,008
for describing expeditions in Wikidata, and

4288
06:04:13,068 --> 06:04:14,468
linking expeditions

4289
06:04:15,808 --> 06:04:17,288
to related entities.

4290
06:04:18,908 --> 06:04:22,608
We use Wikidata as an open, collaborative, and multilingual

4291
06:04:22,828 --> 06:04:25,648
knowledge base for describing expedition metadata,

4292
06:04:26,508 --> 06:04:30,088
and it provides a way to link research expeditions

4293
06:04:31,348 --> 06:04:35,028
to and from collection management systems, data portals,

4294
06:04:35,728 --> 06:04:36,728
and aggregators.

4295
06:04:37,888 --> 06:04:41,408
By proposing a Wikidata schema, providing practical

4296
06:04:41,468 --> 06:04:45,408
examples, and recommending best practices, the task group aims to

4297
06:04:45,448 --> 06:04:48,508
improve the accessibility, discoverability, and

4298
06:04:48,768 --> 06:04:51,948
contextualization of expedition-related data.

4299
